<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lier, Belgium</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lier is a city in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, where the Grote and Kleine Nete rivers meet. A cloth-making town of the Duchy of Brabant in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-handsome">Philip the Handsome</a> married Joanna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1496. Its sights include the Gothic church of St Gummarus and the Zimmer Tower with its astronomical clock.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lier-belgium.jpg" alt="Lier, Belgium" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trougnouf (Benoit Brummer) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hoogbrug_in_Lier,_Belgium_(DSCF0668).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its four chief towns, Leuven, Brussels, Antwerp and 's-Hertogenbosch, along with smaller centres such as Tienen, Nivelles and <b>Lier</b>, grew rich on woollen cloth woven from English wool, and their guilds and patrician families became both partners and rivals of the dukes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
